WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Creating a Defect Opportunity Matrix

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.966
Welcome to this session on defect opportunities.

3
00:00:07.966 --> 00:00:13.166
In this session, we’re going to take a look at the Golden Spur Ranch here.

4
00:00:13.166 --> 00:00:16.466
And to help us understand defect opportunities.

5
00:00:16.466 --> 00:00:23.199
What I've done, is I've identified a number of items given in column B. I'll blow that up for us.

6
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.100
In this case we're looking at people,

7
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.466
horses, dogs, goats, fish, cattle, tractors, equipment, trailers,

8
00:00:30.466 --> 00:00:37.066
cars, motorcycles, structural, plumbing, heating, cooling, floors, irrigation, water, plants,

9
00:00:37.066 --> 00:00:41.066
trees, tack, security, waste.

10
00:00:41.066 --> 00:00:45.066
Now, all those things have to have activities.

11
00:00:45.066 --> 00:00:50.299
We have the operation of, the maintenance of, the cleaning of, the purchasing of.

12
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.300
For example, the purchasing of food.

13
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.533
The licensing of trailers and cars.

14
00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:03.899
The repair of electrical, mechanical things.

15
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:10.800
As I put an estimate in here. Let's say for example, I believe that

16
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:17.733
related to the maintenance of horses

17
00:01:17.733 --> 00:01:21.399
that there is about 50 things that need to be done.

18
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:27.000
Therefore, there are 50 maintenance opportunities where horses are concerned.

19
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.833
Then I can look at, say, for example, the licensing of dogs.

20
00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:35.833
I know that we've got six dogs around here. They all need collars.

21
00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:39.599
So, that's another 10 opportunities.

22
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.266
In other words this, there's 10 opportunities for something to go wrong.

23
00:01:44.266 --> 00:01:49.499
Whoops, excuse me, I put 10 in, I meant six. I’ve got too many dogs.

24
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:56.500
There would be six opportunities, for not getting licenses.

25
00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.900
Each year.

26
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:07.900
There would be 50 opportunities to not properly maintain the horses.

27
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:13.366
Now, that's an example. Let’s take another one.

28
00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:16.866
Automobiles, licenses.

29
00:02:16.866 --> 00:02:22.699
Equipment, tractors. The maintenance of those tractors.

30
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.166
In this case there's a big one and a small one.

31
00:02:26.166 --> 00:02:33.132
Insurance for them, see. The law requires that we have an insurance card in every vehicle.

32
00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:36.099
So, that's an opportunity for something to go wrong.

33
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:41.933
So, I can carefully sit down and think through this, over a couple of hours and a cup of coffee.

34
00:02:41.933 --> 00:02:46.066
And identify, across the whole matrix,

35
00:02:46.066 --> 00:02:49.799
the total number of opportunities

36
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:56.833
for defect that exists in a year or a week or a month or quarterly,

37
00:02:56.833 --> 00:02:59.833
at the Golden Spur Ranch.

38
00:02:59.833 --> 00:03:05.766
Then I could create check sheets. Every time I see something mark it.

39
00:03:05.766 --> 00:03:09.766
This failed to happen. That didn't get done.

40
00:03:09.766 --> 00:03:14.632
Then I can get my defects per opportunity, convert that to a Sigma.

41
00:03:14.633 --> 00:03:18.266
There is another example. At the personal level,

42
00:03:18.266 --> 00:03:21.399
using the Golden Spur Ranch as an example.

43
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:26.400
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
